<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the convergence performance of the d16 and d20 models before and after performing the performance alignment procedure in <a href="#A2.T9" title="In B.5 The Effectiveness of Performance Alignment ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Details ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, demonstrating the effectiveness of this operation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Implementation Techniques</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce several techniques we use in our pipeline.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Computing Score Function with Probability Vector</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we derive <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2.1 Teacher AR as a Conditional Score Model ‣ 3.2 Conditional Score Distillation Loss ‣ 3 Distilled Decoding 2 ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> starting from the preliminaries of flow matching.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> defines an invertible transformation with ordinary differential equation (ODE) <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}x=V(x_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}x=V(x_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math> between two distributions <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The velocity function under linear noise schedule <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be given as:</p>
<table id="A3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(x_{t},t)=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1}\sim\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})}(x_{1}-x_{0}|(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}=x_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(x_{t},t)=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1}\sim\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})}(x_{1}-x_{0}|(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}=x_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is any joint distribution that satisfies temporal boundary conditions at both ends: <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})\mathrm{d}x_{0}=\pi_{1}(x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})\mathrm{d}x_{0}=\pi_{1}(x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})\mathrm{d}x_{1}=\pi_{0}(x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})\mathrm{d}x_{1}=\pi_{0}(x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since the source distribution <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Gaussian distribution here, the relationship between the score function <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> and the velocity <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> is as follows:</p>
<table id="A3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(x_{t},t)=-\frac{ts(x_{t},t)+x_{t}}{1-t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(x_{t},t)=-\frac{ts(x_{t},t)+x_{t}}{1-t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">And the score function can be given as:</p>
<table id="A3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(x_{t},t)=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1}\sim\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})}(-\frac{x_{1}}{t}|(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}=x_{t})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(x_{t},t)=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1}\sim\pi_{0,1}(x_{0},x_{1})}(-\frac{x_{1}}{t}|(1-t)x_{0}+tx_{1}=x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In our problem, the target distribution is a weighted sum of Dirac functions: <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}(x)=\sum_{j=1}^{V}p_{j}\delta(x-c_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}(x)=\sum_{j=1}^{V}p_{j}\delta(x-c_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, and is independent of the source distribution, resulting in only a finite number of possibilities. Therefore, we only need compute the product of the source distribution probability and the target distribution probability for each possible case, and then use this as the weight function to compute the expectation of <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\frac{x_{1}}{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{x_{1}}{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. With the above explanations, we can easily arrive at <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2.1 Teacher AR as a Conditional Score Model ‣ 3.2 Conditional Score Distillation Loss ‣ 3 Distilled Decoding 2 ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Multiple Noisy Samples for Fake Conditional Score Learning</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 Training Objective ‣ 3.2 Conditional Score Distillation Loss ‣ 3 Distilled Decoding 2 ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>,
the output of the guidance network <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{\psi}(q^{t_{i}}_{i},_{i}|q_{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\psi}(q^{t_{i}}_{i},_{i}|q_{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math> is computed in two stages. First, a transformer backbone process the input sequence <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs a feature sequence <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},\ldots,f_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},\ldots,f_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is causally conditioned on <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{&lt;i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{&lt;i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, a lightweight MLP takes the noisy token <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{t_{i}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{t_{i}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, timestep <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the feature <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as input, then outputs the estimated conditional score <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\psi}(q^{t_{i}}_{i},t_{i}|q_{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\psi}(q^{t_{i}}_{i},t_{i}|q_{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Every conditioning <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a continuous distribution over noisy inputs <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the model must learn to predict score function across the entire space and all timesteps. To ensure sufficient training and improve generalization, we draw inspiration from the MAR implementation (see <a href="https://github.com/LTH14/mar" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/LTH14/mar</a> for more details) and apply a multi-sample training strategy. Specifically, for a generated sequence <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample multiple noise sequences <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{1},\ldots,\epsilon_{n})_{1,\ldots,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{1},\ldots,\epsilon_{n})_{1,\ldots,m}</annotation></semantics></math> to create multiple noisy versions of the generated sequence. For each noisy sequence, we apply <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2.3 Learning the Conditional Score of the Generator ‣ 3.2 Conditional Score Distillation Loss ‣ 3 Distilled Decoding 2 ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> as the loss function and take the average across all <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> samples. The resulting training objective is:</p>
<table id="A3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{FCS}=\mathbb{E}_{(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})\sim p_{\theta}}\sum^{m}_{j=1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|s_{\psi}(q^{t_{i,j}}_{i},t_{i,j}|q_{&lt;i})-\nabla_{q^{t_{i,j}}_{i}}\log p(q^{t_{i,j}}_{i}|q_{i})\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{FCS}=\mathbb{E}_{(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})\sim p_{\theta}}\sum^{m}_{j=1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|s_{\psi}(q^{t_{i,j}}_{i},t_{i,j}|q_{&lt;i})-\nabla_{q^{t_{i,j}}_{i}}\log p(q^{t_{i,j}}_{i}|q_{i})\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample at the <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token position, <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{t_{i,j}}_{i}=(1-t_{i,j})q_{i}+t_{i,j}\epsilon_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{t_{i,j}}_{i}=(1-t_{i,j})q_{i}+t_{i,j}\epsilon_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0;\textbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">I</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0;\textbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th noise sample at the <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token position.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Performance Alignment for both Generator and Guidance network</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We find there are two issues during training: <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> there is a discrepancy between the guidance network and the generator’s score, and <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> unstable training dynamics. Specifically, we observe that the samples generated by the conditional guidance network via AR-diffusion tend to under-perform those generated by the generator, indicating a training gap of the guidance network. Additionally, we notice that the generator’s FID fluctuates significantly during training. However, applying the Exponential Moving Average (EMA) technique to the generator leads to a much more stable performance curve. These findings motivate us to introduce a <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">performance alignment</span> procedure for both generator and guidance network after an initial phase of training. Specifically, <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> for the generator, we replace the regular model weights directly with EMA weights, then <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> we fix the generator and only train the conditional guidance network for a certain period to adapt it to the new generator distribution. Once this alignment process is complete, we resume the standard training process. We empirically found that this technique is particularly helpful for training VAR-d16 and VAR-d20 models, significantly improving performance even after the models have already converged. Results are shown at <a href="#A2.T9" title="In B.5 The Effectiveness of Performance Alignment ‣ Appendix B More Experimental Details ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Larger Update Frequency for the Guidance Network</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training the guidance network is crucial, as it is responsible for producing accurate conditional scores of the generator distribution. However, this task is challenging because the generator distribution is also evolving during training. To address this issue, we adopt a higher update frequency for the guidance network, following the strategy used in DMD2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. Specifically, in each training iteration, we update the guidance network <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> times with (<math id="A3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) while updating the generator only once. The specific values of <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> used for different models are provided in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Detailed Experimental Settings ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">App.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5 </span>Details of Model Architectures</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the same architecture for both generator and guidance network. Inspired by MAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, our model architecture consists of a transformer backbone and a lightweight MLP head. As discussed in <a href="#S3" title="3 Distilled Decoding 2 ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, for the guidance network, the transformer backbone takes the token sequence as input and output a causal feature sequence, while the MLP head takes the feature, noisy token and timestep as input and output the predicted conditional score. For the generator, the transformer backbone takes the shifted noise sequence as input, while the MLP head takes the noise sequence and the feature sequence as input and give the final generated sequence. We demonstrate the model architectures in <a href="#A3.F7" title="In C.5 Details of Model Architectures ‣ Appendix C Implementation Techniques ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.21003v1/tex/fig/Arch.png" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/219;" width="429" height="219" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A3.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Demonstration of the model architectures and the corresponding inputs/outputs.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Algorithms of Multi-step Sampling Method</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the pseudo algorithm of the multi-step sampling method in <a href="#S3" title="3 Distilled Decoding 2 ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Suppose we have a sequence <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the indexing operations <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X[t]=q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[t]=q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X[:t]=(q_{1},\ldots,q_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[:t]=(q_{1},\ldots,q_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The pseudo algorithm is presented in <a href="#alg4" title="In Appendix D Algorithms of Multi-step Sampling Method ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Alg.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, with results reported in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In 5.3 Results of Multi-step Sampling ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Distilled Decoding 2: One-step Sampling of Image Auto-regressive Models with Conditional Score Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg4" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 4</span> </span>Sampling with the teacher AR model</figcaption>
<div id="alg4.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg4.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="alg4.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">




















































<span id="alg4.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_listing">
<span id="alg4.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:89%;">0:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 : The distilled one-step model <math id="alg4.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, the pre-trained AR model <math id="alg4.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, total sampling steps <math id="alg4.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.

</span>
<span id="alg4.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_listingline">
</span>
<span id="alg4.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:89%;">0:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 
</span>
<span id="alg4.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg4.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:89%;">1:</span></span>
                  
                  
                 <math id="alg4.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(q_{1},\ldots,q_{n})\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> one-step sampling by <math id="alg4.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>

</span>
